+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` lives in `src/patch.ts` and already exports `isObject`, `clone` and `setOwn` helpers; `src/pointer.ts` parses pointers but does not build them. See proposal.md for motivation.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Correct round trip with `applyPatch` for every JSON input.
+- Patches proportional to the change, with array edits at item level.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- A provably shortest patch; `copy` and `test` operations are never emitted.
+- Detecting moves of items across different containers.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Objects**: iterate own keys (`Object.keys` / `Object.hasOwn`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary member); emit `remove`, then recurse on shared keys, then `add`.
+- **Arrays**: compute a longest common subsequence of items using deep equality. Items outside it are removals/insertions. Within a gap between matched items holding both removals and insertions, pair them by position and recurse (so an edited item becomes a nested change); surplus ones become `remove` / `add`. A move naturally becomes `remove` + `add`, which the spec allows; no `move` is emitted. Alternative considered: index-wise comparison - rejected because an insertion would rewrite every following item.
+- **Index bookkeeping**: operations are emitted in order against the progressively-edited array, tracking a running index so each path is valid when applied.
+- **Safety**: all values placed in the patch are cloned via `clone`; inputs are only read. Pointer tokens are escaped (`~` → `~0`, `/` → `~1`).
+- Reuse `equal`/`clone`/`isObject` by exporting `equal` from `patch.ts`.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [LCS is O(n·m) for arrays] → Trim common prefix and suffix first so typical edits are cheap.
+- [Remove+add for moves is longer than `move` for large items] → Acceptable per requirements; correctness first.
